verdict:
The design is mostly complete for the default anonymous deploy, but it is not yet a full answer to "the initial site should work completely". I grepped src/ for session, sign-in, Prisma, feature-flag and /api fetch calls. Every hard blocker an anonymous visitor hits today is addressed:
- the lab-page redirect to sign-in;
- the silent 401s from Teach-it-back and the AI widget;
- the dashboard, notebook and settings 404 gates;
- SessionProvider polling /api/auth/session;
- every "Sign in" string in LabLink, PracticeHub, ModuleCard, LessonPractice, learning-paths.ts and the home card.

What is left falls into five groups:
1. Pages and copy the design does not mention, which still show placeholder or misleading content.
2. A server/client flag divergence that can cause hydration mismatches.
3. The re-enable path: once accounts are switched on, signed-out visitors would again hit sign-in walls and failing AI and simulation controls, and signed-in learners would appear to lose their local progress.
4. localStorage quota and concurrency risks that can lose data.
5. Tests and files with no owner. The computed task text cuts off partway through WP1, so I could check file ownership only for WP0 and WP1. Ownership for WP2–WP7 is unverified, and the listed files must be claimed there.

I ran vitest on tests/security-config.test.ts, tests/app/feature-gating.spec.tsx and tests/app/sitemap.test.ts. 16 tests pass and 1 already fails: security-config's production case expects the auth route import to throw.
